---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    route={['Spain', 'Andalucía', 'Seville']}
    label="Guide · Seven Excursions"
    title="Best Day Trips from Seville:"
    titleEmphasis="Seven Assessed, Two Refused"
    standfirst="Distance is a poor guide to what makes a good day out of Seville. Ronda is closer than Córdoba and four times harder to reach. What matters is the shape of the day the railway lets you have."
    stats={[
      { num: '7', label: 'Assessed' },
      { num: '45 min', label: 'Nearest by Rail' },
      { num: '5', label: 'Shapes of Day' },
      { num: '1', label: 'Better as an Overnight' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Seville sits at the centre of the densest concentration of interesting
          ground in southern Europe, and the standard list of excursions from it
          is more or less fixed: Córdoba, Cádiz, Granada, Ronda, and then
          whichever of Jerez, Itálica or Carmona a particular writer happens to
          favour. What the standard list rarely does is decide between them, or
          admit that two of the seven make poor days out and one makes an
          excellent night.
        </p>
        <p>
          The reason the list needs deciding is that distance predicts almost
          nothing here. Ronda is a hundred and thirty kilometres from Seville and
          Córdoba is a hundred and forty, and the difference in a day is
          enormous: Córdoba is forty-five minutes on the high-speed line and
          Ronda is two and a half hours by coach or three and a half by a train
          that goes round three sides of a mountain. Itálica is twelve kilometres
          away and takes half an hour, because it needs a suburban bus. What
          governs an Andalusian day trip is not how far the place is. It is which
          of three lines out of the city it happens to sit on.
        </p>
        <p>
          So this page is organised by the shape of the day rather than by the
          merit of the destination — half day, comfortable full day, full day that
          earns itself, very long day, and better as an overnight. Three of the
          seven are documented on this site from a stay recorded as
          <a href="/10-days-in-sevilla/">ten days using Seville as a base</a>:
          Córdoba and Cádiz have chronicles of their own, and Granada was made as
          a day trip from Seville on that stay. <strong>The remaining four —
          Jerez, Itálica, Carmona and Ronda — have not been visited from Seville
          and are assessed rather than remembered.</strong> Where the difference
          matters, the text says which is which. It assumes no car, because a car
          changes every answer on the page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/mezquita-arch-within-arch.jpg`}
      alt="Inside the Mezquita-Catedral of Córdoba: a tall Christian Gothic pointed arch in the foreground framing the interlacing red-and-white polylobed Islamic arches and carved plasterwork of the Villaviciosa chapel beyond, with a crucifix below and coloured light from stained glass thrown across the stone floor."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="One frame, two religions, and the whole argument for giving Córdoba a day: a Gothic arch looking through into the Islamic hall it was inserted beside. Nothing on this page comes close to it."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="who-for" rule={false}>The three lines out of Seville</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Everything below sits on one of three axes, and the axis explains the
        verdict better than the destination does.
        <strong>North-east, up the Guadalquivir valley:</strong> Itálica at twelve
        kilometres, Carmona at thirty-three, Córdoba at a hundred and forty on the
        high-speed line. <strong>South-west, down the line to the Atlantic:</strong>
        Jerez at ninety, Cádiz at a hundred and twenty-five, on the same frequent
        service. <strong>East, into the mountains:</strong> Ronda and Granada,
        where the rail geography stops cooperating entirely. The first two axes
        are the reason day trips from Seville are unusually rewarding. The third
        is the reason two of them are not.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AnchorNav
      links={[
        { id: 'italica', label: 'Itálica · half day' },
        { id: 'cordoba', label: 'Córdoba · full day' },
        { id: 'jerez', label: 'Jerez · full day' },
        { id: 'carmona', label: 'Carmona · full day' },
        { id: 'cadiz', label: 'Cádiz · a day it earns' },
        { id: 'granada', label: 'Granada · a very long day' },
        { id: 'ronda', label: 'Ronda · sleep there' },
        { id: 'choose', label: 'How to choose' },
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <AtAGlance
