      { num: 'Cádiz', text: 'Full day · ~1 h 45 by train · Three thousand years on an Atlantic spit. A day it earns rather than fills.' },
      { num: 'Granada', text: 'Very long day · 2½–3 h+ each way · The Alhambra, and nothing else. Tickets months ahead or do not go.' },
      { num: 'Ronda', text: 'Overnight · 2–2½ h by bus, 3 h 20 by train · Magnificent, and wrong as a day trip. Sleep there instead.' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter
      id="italica"
      number="I."
      name="The Half Day"
      epithet="Itálica — a Roman city twelve kilometres out, and the best value on this page"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Before Seville there was Hispalis, and beside Hispalis there was Itálica:
        founded in 206 BC for the veterans of a campaign against Carthage, grown
        into a provincial capital, and the birthplace of two Roman emperors,
        Trajan and Hadrian. It stands at Santiponce, twelve kilometres north-west
        of the city, and what survives is a street grid, mosaic floors still in
        their houses, a theatre, and an amphitheatre built for something like
        twenty-five thousand people — one of the largest in the empire, and
        emptier on a Tuesday morning than any comparable Roman site in Spain.
        Television has found it: the amphitheatre stood in for the dragonpit in
        the later seasons of <em>Game of Thrones</em>, which brings a certain kind
        of visitor and has not yet brought crowds.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is the only genuine half day on this list, and the only one that costs
        essentially nothing. A metropolitan bus from Plaza de Armas takes about
        half an hour; two hours on the site is generous, three is thorough, and
        you are back in Seville for lunch. That makes it the right answer for a
        traveller with a spare morning rather than a spare day — and the
        right answer for anyone who is going to visit the Palacio de la Condesa de
        Lebrija in the city, whose drawing-room floors are Roman mosaics lifted
        from these ruins in the 1900s. Seeing the ruins first turns that house from
        a curiosity into something closer to an accusation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Getting there</strong> metropolitan bus from Plaza de Armas,
        roughly 30 minutes; confirm the current line and timetable with the
        Consorcio de Transportes rather than a guide ·
        <strong>Time on site</strong> 2 to 3 hours ·
        <strong>Practicalities</strong> free to EU residents, otherwise about
        €1.50. Tuesday to Saturday 09:00–18:00; Sundays, public holidays and
        Mondays that precede a holiday 09:00–15:00;
        <strong>closed on ordinary Mondays</strong>. Andalusian archaeological
        sites generally shorten their hours through high summer, so check before a
        July or August visit · <strong>Verdict</strong> yes, and the easiest yes
        here. <em>Assessed rather than visited from Seville.</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="cordoba"
      number="II."
      name="The Comfortable Full Days"
      epithet="Córdoba, Jerez and Carmona — three destinations the transport genuinely wants you to visit"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="cordoba-entry" level={3} rule={false}>Córdoba — the one to make if you make only one</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Córdoba was, in the tenth century, plausibly the largest city in Europe
        and the seat of a caliphate whose libraries and mathematics had no
        northern equal. What remains of that is one building, and it is enough to
        organise a day around and probably a trip. The Mezquita-Catedral begins as
        a forest: hundreds of columns of jasper and marble carrying red-and-white
        striped double arches that recede in every direction, so that the eye
        cannot find a wall and the mind cannot settle on a scale. Into the middle
        of that forest, in the sixteenth century, the cathedral chapter inserted a
        complete Gothic church — a decision their contemporaries called
        desecration and which the emperor who authorised it is said to have
        regretted. The result is not beautiful in any ordinary sense. It is
        stranger than beautiful, and it is the whole history of Andalucía made
        architectural.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rest of the day is easy and good: the Judería, the only surviving
        medieval synagogue in Andalucía, the flowered lanes, the arcaded Plaza de
        la Corredera, and the Puente Romano over a Guadalquivir that here runs
        wide and shallow over weirs with reed islands in it. The day trip
        documented on this site departed Sevilla-Santa Justa at a quarter past
        seven, was in Córdoba by five past ten, and came back on the half past
        eight train — eight hours in the city, which is the right amount.
      </p>
      <p>
        What this page will not do is tell you how to spend those eight hours,
        because
        <a href="/1-day-in-cordoba/">there is a full account of one day in Córdoba</a>
        on this site already, written from that visit. Read it after you have
        decided, not instead of deciding.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Getting there</strong> AVE from Sevilla-Santa Justa, about 45
        minutes; slower services take around an hour and a half. Book ahead —
        the fast trains fill · <strong>Time on site</strong> 6 to 8 hours ·
        <strong>Practicalities</strong> the Mezquita takes timed entry booked
        online, opens at 10:00, and deserves two hours minimum. The synagogue
        closes on Mondays. Start early: Córdoba is hotter than Seville in high
        summer and the Mezquita is the only air-conditioned part of the day ·
        <strong>Verdict</strong> the strongest day trip from Seville, without
        qualification. <em>Documented.</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/cordoba-puente-romano.jpg`}
      alt="The Puente Romano at Córdoba in low afternoon light: a long honey-coloured stone bridge on wide arches with rounded pier buttresses, people walking along its parapet, the battlemented Torre de la Calahorra at the far end, and the shallow Guadalquivir running over weirs between reed islands."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Five o'clock at the Roman bridge, with the Calahorra tower at the far end. The river here is wide, shallow and full of islands — nothing like the deep working channel at Seville."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="jerez" level={3}>Jerez de la Frontera — the trip nobody takes</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Fifty minutes down the Atlantic line, and the most overlooked destination
        on this page. Jerez gave sherry its name — the English word is a mangling
        of the Arabic <em>Sherish</em> — and the wine is made here by a method
        that is genuinely unlike anything in Bordeaux or Burgundy: the solera, a
        cascade of barrels in which older wine is continuously refreshed by
        younger, so that no bottle is of any single year and the cellar itself
        becomes the vintage. The bodegas are cathedral-scaled, cool, and open to
        visitors. It is the best possible use of an Andalusian afternoon in July.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other half of Jerez is horses. The Real Escuela Andaluza del Arte
        Ecuestre keeps a school of Andalusian stallions in a nineteenth-century
        estate on the edge of town and performs an exhibition — a kind of
        choreographed dressage, which sounds absurd on paper and is not — on
        selected days of the week. On the other days the estate can be visited for
        the training sessions, the stables, the carriage museum and the saddlery.
        The show days and times move by season and are not published far
        ahead: check the school's own calendar, and book, before building a day
        around it. Beyond those two things there is a Moorish alcázar, a cathedral
        on a rise above the vineyards, and an old town that is close to empty on
        a weekday.
      </p>
      <p>
        Jerez also solves a problem, which is the strongest argument for it. It is
        a stop on the Cádiz line, so a traveller who has already given a day to
        Cádiz can take Jerez as a second, quite different Atlantic day without
        repeating any of the ground.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Getting there</strong> Renfe from Sevilla-Santa Justa, about 47 to
        52 minutes, with something like seventeen services a day ·
        <strong>Time on site</strong> 6 to 7 hours ·
        <strong>Practicalities</strong> bodega visits are guided, generally need
        booking, and often close on Sundays. The equestrian exhibition runs on
        selected days only — confirm on the official calendar and buy in advance.
        Do not attempt Jerez and Cádiz in one day: the train makes it look
        possible and both places deserve better ·
        <strong>Verdict</strong> yes, for anyone interested in wine, horses, or
        an Andalusian town without a coach park. <em>Assessed rather than
        visited.</em>
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="carmona" level={3}>Carmona — a walled town on a ridge</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Thirty-three kilometres east, on a ridge above the farmland, Carmona has
        been fortified by every power that has held this valley: Carthaginians,
        Romans, Moors, and Castilians who built a fortress on the highest point
        that is now a parador. Two things make it more than a pretty walled town.
        The Roman necropolis on its western edge is a field of chambered tombs cut
        into the rock, several of them large enough to walk down into, with
        painted walls and niches for urns — an unusually intact piece of Roman
        domestic religion. And the Puerta de Sevilla is not a gate but a small
        fortress with a Carthaginian base, Roman upper works and a Moorish
        courtyard stacked on top of one another, which can be climbed.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is the quietest destination on this list. It is also the one where the
        transport is least generous: a metropolitan bus rather than a train, about
        an hour for the full run, with a separate timetable through July and
        August. That makes Carmona a short full day rather than a comfortable one
        — five or six hours in the town, and a bus at each end that you should
        look up properly rather than trust to frequency.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Getting there</strong> metropolitan bus line M-124 from Seville,
        operated by CASAL, roughly an hour end to end; the Seville terminus is
        Plaza de Armas. The line has variants and a separate summer timetable, so
        check the Consorcio de Transportes listing for your date ·
        <strong>Time on site</strong> 5 to 6 hours ·
        <strong>Practicalities</strong> the necropolis and the gate-fortress both
        keep museum hours and Monday closures are common in Andalucía; confirm
        both. Little shade on the ridge ·
        <strong>Verdict</strong> yes, for a second or third visit to the region,
        or for anyone who has had enough of queues. <em>Assessed rather than
        visited.</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <Beat>Two railway lines and a bus route, and everything the transport likes is worth going to.</Beat>

    <Chapter
      id="cadiz"
      number="III."
      name="The Day That Earns Itself"
      epithet="Cádiz — an hour and three quarters each way, three thousand years, and a different Spain entirely"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Cádiz is the oldest continuously inhabited city in western Europe by most
        reckonings — Phoenician, then Carthaginian, then Roman, then a port that
        received the silver of the Americas and was raided for it by the English
        and the Dutch. It stands on a spit of Atlantic shore so narrow that the
        old city is walled by the sea on three sides, and the entire historic
        centre can be walked in a day without hurrying. Its cathedral was built
        over a hundred and sixteen years on New World money and changed style
        twice while it went up. Its beach, La Caleta, is a crescent of sand cupped
        between two sea forts.
      </p>
      <p>
        An hour and three quarters each way is a real cost, and the reason to pay
        it is that Cádiz is not a smaller Seville. Seville is inland, imperial and
        landlocked in temperament, a city that grew rich on an ocean it could not
        see. Cádiz is maritime, salt-bitten and open on every side, and the light
        and the air and the food are all different. Nothing else on this page
        gives a traveller a second Spain for the price of a train.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full day is documented on this site as
        <a href="/1-day-in-cadiz/">one day in Cádiz</a>, walked in October from the
        morning train, and it is the account to read once the decision is made.
        The relevant decision here is only this: Cádiz is a full day and rewards
        being treated as one. The afternoon day-trippers who arrive at two and
        leave at six get the cathedral and a coffee and miss the city entirely.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Getting there</strong> Renfe from Sevilla-Santa Justa, about an
        hour and three quarters, hourly through the day; the same line stops at
        Jerez. On the documented visit in 2025 the fare was around €12–18 each
        way, which is a 2025 observation rather than a current quote ·
        <strong>Time on site</strong> 8 to 10 hours; the last useful train back
        leaves in the evening ·
        <strong>Practicalities</strong> everything is on foot — do not take a car
        inside the walls. The Museum of Cádiz is free to EU citizens. La Caleta
        faces west, so if you can stay for the Atlantic sunset from the sea wall,
        do · <strong>Verdict</strong> the best second day trip from Seville, and
        the best first one for anyone who has already seen Córdoba.
        <em>Documented.</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/cadiz-cathedral-facade.jpg`}
      alt="The west front of Cádiz Cathedral: a broad pale limestone baroque façade with two domed bell towers, a large arched central bay and a carved doorway, half of it in shadow and half in golden morning light, with palm fronds crossing the top of the frame."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Cádiz Cathedral took a hundred and sixteen years and three architectural fashions to finish, and was paid for with silver landed a few hundred metres away."
    />

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/cadiz-la-caleta.jpg`}
      alt="La Caleta beach in Cádiz: a domed white balneario building raised on piers at the left, a long stone breakwater running out towards a sea fort, turquoise Atlantic water, and bathers and sunbathers on pale sand."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="La Caleta, held between two forts. This is the argument for Cádiz in one frame — nothing in Seville looks remotely like it, or smells like it either."
    />

    <Chapter
      id="granada"
      number="IV."
      name="The Very Long Day"
      epithet="Granada — defensible for one building, and only if the tickets are already bought"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The Alhambra is not primarily a palace. It is an enclosed Nasrid city on a
        red hill between the plain of Granada and the first ridges of the Sierra
        Nevada: a fortress at the western end, the Palacios Nazaríes in the
        centre, and the Generalife summer palaces and their gardens at the east.
        The water everywhere — channels, fountains, reflecting pools — is not
        decoration. It is an engineering achievement and a philosophical
        statement at once, because in a dry country the control of water was the
        most eloquent expression of power available. It is the finest surviving
        building of Islamic Spain and one of the two or three most extraordinary
        places in Europe.
      </p>
      <p>
        And it is two and a half to three hours from Seville on the fastest
        services, appreciably longer on the rest — the average across the day's
        eleven or so trains runs past three and a half hours, and the day trip
        documented on this site took a service that ran three and a half hours
        each way, leaving at seven in the morning and returning on the 18:55.
        Faster trains now exist; slower ones still do too, and that spread is the
        whole problem. On the worst plausible combination a Granada day trip is
        seven hours of travelling for four hours in the city.
      </p>
      <p>
        The verdict is therefore conditional and the condition is absolute.
        <strong>The Nasrid Palaces take a timed ticket that sells out months
        ahead in season.</strong> The ten-day chronicle on this site puts it
        without hedging: arriving in Granada without pre-purchased Nasrid Palaces
        entry means you will be looking at the outside of a wall. If you have
        those tickets, and you accept a fourteen-hour day, and you understand that
        you will see the Alhambra and the Mirador de San Nicolás and essentially
        nothing else of a substantial city, then go — it is worth it, and the
        return through the Andalusian dark is part of the day rather than a
        penalty. If any of those three conditions fails, do not.
      </p>
      <p>
        The better answer, for anyone whose dates allow it, is not to make Granada
        a day trip at all but to move there for two nights on the way east. That
        is how it appears in the wider regional routes on this site rather than as
        an excursion.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Getting there</strong> Renfe from Sevilla-Santa Justa; fastest
        around two and a half hours, typically nearer three, and considerably more
        on some services. Book at least a fortnight ahead ·
        <strong>Time on site</strong> 6 to 7 hours on a good day ·
        <strong>Practicalities</strong> book the Alhambra the moment your dates
        are fixed, through the official channel, and allow three to four hours for
        the full complex. Entry to the Nasrid Palaces is by half-hour slot and
        strictly enforced. One consolation the rest of Andalucía does not offer:
        Granada still gives a free tapa with every drink ordered, which is a
        different food culture from
        <a href="/seville-tapas-food-guide/">Seville's, where tapas are ordered and paid for</a> ·
        <strong>Verdict</strong> a qualified yes, for the Alhambra alone.
        <em>Documented as a day trip on the recorded stay.</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="ronda"
      number="V."
      name="The One to Sleep In"
      epithet="Ronda — magnificent, closer than Córdoba, and the wrong shape for a day"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Ronda stands on a limestone plateau split by the Tajo, a gorge a hundred
        metres deep, with the eighteenth-century Puente Nuevo thrown across it and
        the old town on one side and the new on the other. It holds one of the
        oldest bullrings in Spain, the Arab baths, a clifftop walk with a hundred
        kilometres of sierra in front of it, and the ruins of the Roman town of
        Acinipo half an hour further out. It is one of the most photographed
        places in Spain and it deserves to be.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also the destination on this page that a day trip serves worst, and
        the arithmetic is straightforward. The train from Seville takes around
        three hours and twenty minutes, which is longer than Granada; the coach
        takes between two hours and two and a half and is the practical option.
        Either way you spend four to five hours travelling to reach a town whose
        two best hours are the ones a day-tripper cannot have — early morning on
        the bridge before the coaches arrive from the Costa del Sol, and the hour
        after sunset when the gorge goes blue and the day visitors have gone. A
        day trip to Ronda delivers you at the busiest moment and removes you
        before the good one.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the verdict is not that Ronda is unworthy. It is that Ronda is worth
        more than a day trip can extract from it. Take a night there — it is
        small, the accommodation includes some of the more remarkable rooms in
        Andalucía on the gorge edge, and it works naturally as a stop between
        Seville and Málaga or Granada rather than as an out-and-back. Ronda has
        not been visited from Seville for this site, and no chronicle of it exists
        here yet; that assessment is made on the transport and on what the town is
        like at which hours, not on a remembered day.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Getting there</strong> coach with Damas, about two hours to two
        and a half; train around three hours twenty, by a slower route ·
        <strong>Time on site</strong> 4 to 5 hours as a day trip, which is the
        problem · <strong>Practicalities</strong> if you go anyway, take the
        earliest coach out and the latest back, and accept that the middle of your
        day is the middle of everyone else's ·
        <strong>Verdict</strong> go, but sleep there. Not a day trip.
        <em>Assessed rather than visited from Seville.</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <Practical place="Day Trips from Seville" detail="Seven destinations · verified 29 July 2026" detailIcon="🚆">
      <PracticalSection label="By shape of day" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Half day</strong> — Itálica. ~30 min by metropolitan bus from
          Plaza de Armas. Free to EU residents. Closed ordinary Mondays.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Comfortable full day</strong> — Córdoba (~45 min by AVE) ·
          Jerez (~50 min by Renfe) · Carmona (~1 h by bus M-124).
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Full day, treated as one</strong> — Cádiz (~1 h 45 by Renfe).
          Not an afternoon.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Very long day, conditional</strong> — Granada (2½ h at best,
          often more). Alhambra tickets in hand, or do not go.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Overnight, not a day trip</strong> — Ronda (~2–2½ h by coach).
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Booking, in priority order" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Alhambra Nasrid Palaces</strong> — the moment your dates are
          fixed, months ahead in season, through the official channel. No other
          booking on this page is remotely as urgent.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Mezquita-Catedral, Córdoba</strong> — timed entry online; opens
          at 10:00; allow two hours minimum.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Jerez equestrian exhibition</strong> — selected days only, and
          the days move by season. Check the school's own calendar and buy in
          advance; a bodega visit needs booking too.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Fast trains</strong> — Córdoba's AVE and Granada's services fill
          in season. Book at least a week out, a fortnight for Granada.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Nothing needed</strong> — Itálica, Carmona and Cádiz can all be
          done on a walk-up ticket bought the same morning.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Watch-outs" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Mondays close things across Andalucía.</strong> Itálica shuts on
          ordinary Mondays, Córdoba's synagogue shuts, and municipal museums in
          Carmona and Jerez commonly do too. A Monday is the day to stay in
          Seville — where Casa de Pilatos, the Alcázar and the Cathedral all open.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Journey times quoted here are bands, not timetables.</strong>
          Seville–Granada in particular ranges from about two and a half hours to
          well over three depending on the service, and the difference decides
          whether the day works. Check the actual departure before you commit.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Do not combine two destinations in one day.</strong> Jerez and
          Cádiz sit on the same line and the temptation is obvious; the result is
          two rushed half-days and a lot of platform.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Start early, everywhere.</strong> Every destination on this
          page is hotter than Seville in high summer except Cádiz, which is
          windier. The documented Córdoba day left Santa Justa at 07:15 for good
          reason.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Four of these seven have not been visited from Seville</strong>
          for this site — Jerez, Itálica, Carmona and Ronda. Their sections are
          assessments built from transport data, published site hours and what
          each place is, and they are labelled as such rather than written as
          memory.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>

    <PullQuote>
      Ronda is ten kilometres nearer than Córdoba and four times harder to reach.
      In Andalucía the railway, not the map, decides what a day can contain.
    </PullQuote>

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="choose">How to choose, by how long you have</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        <strong>Three days in Seville: make no day trip at all.</strong> This is
        the recommendation and it is firmer than it looks. Any excursion takes a
        third of a three-day visit and returns a train journey and a second city
        you will see too fast, and Seville itself has not been finished at that
        point — the case is made in
        <a href="/3-days-in-seville/">the three-day route</a>, which deliberately
        excludes all of them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Four or five days: one day trip, and it is Córdoba.</strong>
        Forty-five minutes each way, a building that changes the scale by which
        you measure other buildings, and you are back for dinner in Seville.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Six or seven days: two, and take Cádiz as the second.</strong>
        The pairing works because the two are opposite. Add Itálica as a half day
        if a morning is going spare, since it costs nothing and finishes by lunch.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Eight days or more: three, and here the choice opens.</strong>
        Jerez if wine or horses appeal; Carmona if what you want is a town with
        nobody in it; Granada only if the Alhambra tickets came through and you
        are willing to give it a fourteen-hour day. This is also the trip length
        at which the honest answer may be to stop making day trips and move —
        two nights in Granada, or one in Ronda, in the manner of
        <a href="/4-weeks-portugal-to-spain-by-rail-road/">the longer overland routes</a>
        recorded on this site.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="cut">Considered and cut</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Four destinations that appear on other lists are not assessed above, for
        the same reason in each case: without a car they do not make a day.
        <strong>Sanlúcar de Barrameda</strong> and the Doñana boat crossing need a
        connection at Jerez and a great deal of luck with timings.
        <strong>Osuna</strong> is reachable but thin for a full day.
        <strong>Aracena</strong> and its cave sit two hours north-west on
        infrequent buses. And the white villages of the
        <strong>Sierra de Grazalema</strong> are the best driving in Andalucía and
        essentially unreachable on public transport in a day. A car changes all
        four verdicts, and it changes the audience of this page, so they are named
        here rather than assessed above.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/triana-tiled-excursion-signs.jpg`}
      alt="A wall of old Andalusian trade azulejos: a large yellow and blue tiled panel advertising a six-day coach excursion to Cordoba, Sevilla and Granada departing Madrid every Tuesday and Saturday, surrounded by tiled shop and house signs including E. Gonzalez, El Madrigal, Villa Julita, Hermanas Elvira y Estefania, a Singer sewing-machine roundel and a painted Virgin medallion."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Kept at the Centro Cerámica Triana: a pre-war tiled advertisement for a six-day coach tour of Córdoba, Seville and Granada, departing Madrid every Tuesday and Saturday. The circuit on this page is a century old and was always sold in French."
    />
  </div>

  <div class="post-closing">
    <div class="narrative prose" style="max-width:820px; margin:0 auto;">
      <p>
        The tiled advertisement above hangs in a former tile works in Triana, and
        it is worth a minute because it settles an argument. Córdoba, Seville and
        Granada were being sold as a package, in French, to Madrid coach
        passengers, before either world war. The circuit is not a modern invention
        and neither is the mistake it encourages — six days for three cities, each
        arrived at and departed from, none of them stayed in.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which is the whole of the case for being sparing with day trips. Seville
        rewards return rather than conquest; every day given to a train is a day
        the city does not get, and the two or three excursions that genuinely earn
        their place earn it by being unlike Seville rather than by adding to it.
        Córdoba is a different civilisation's high point. Cádiz is a different
        sea. Itálica is a different empire. Everything else on this page is a
        town, and there will be other years. The rest of the region, taken slowly,
        is gathered in <a href="/chronicles/">the archive</a> and laid out on
        <a href="/by-region/">the map by region</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

